// PIN ZYJS-007 — an identifier that ends in a non-ASCII digit.
//
// The Rust lexer continues an identifier on `is_alphanumeric()`, and `२` — the
// Devanagari two — is alphanumeric. zyjs continued on `0-9` alone, so
// `कार्यस्थितिः२ = अ::प्रतिकृतिः(…)` lexed as an identifier, a NUMBER, and then
// the `=`: three tokens where the language has one name.
//
// It did not fail. `parsePrimary`'s catch-all swallowed the `=` and left a
// statement that did nothing, so Chaturanga's alpha-beta test ran here with a
// wrong parse, correctly under both Rust engines, and nothing said so. Closing
// ZYJS-001 turned the silent mis-parse into a visible refusal, and the refusal
// is what named this.
//
// Found by validating against the LDV applications, not by the corpus: no
// corpus file names a variable in Sanskrit. Closed 2026-08-30.
//
// One script per family the language claims to support, because the rule is
// about the Unicode category and not about Devanagari.
क२ = 5
>> क२ ¶
متغير٣ = 7
>> متغير٣ ¶
变量９ = 11
>> 变量９ ¶
x9 = 1
>> x9 ¶
// And the thing that must NOT change: a digit still STARTS a number, in any
// script, so `२२` is twenty-two and not a name. Written as output rather than
// as `२२ = 0`, which is refused by all three engines in three different
// wordings — a real difference, but not this pin's question, and a pin that
// quietly covers an open one reports it as closed.
>> (२२ + 1) ¶
>> ٣٤ ¶
